<<<
If 0.t.c. is to stand first, it must be in such forms as Food was not abundant; 0. t. c., it was running short.
===
If *o. t. c.* is to stand first, it must be in such forms as *Food was not abundant*; *o. t. c.*, it was running short.
>>>

<<<
contrary, converse, opposite. These are sometimes confused, & occasionally precision is important.
===
**contrary, converse, opposite.** These are sometimes confused, & occasionally precision is important.
>>>

<<<
If we take the statement All men are mortal, its contrary is Not all men are mortal, its converse is All mortal beings are men, & its opposite is No men are mortal.
===
If we take the statement *All men are mortal*, its contrary is *Not all men are mortal*, its converse is *All mortal beings are men*, & its opposite is *No men are mortal*.
>>>

<<<
Thus This is white has only one opposite, This is black, but many contraries, as This is not white, This is coloured, This is dirty, This is black; & whether
===
Thus *This is white* has only one opposite, *This is black*, but many contraries, as *This is not white*, *This is coloured*, *This is dirty*, *This is black*; & whether
>>>

<<<
the last form is called the contrary, or more emphatically the opposite, is usually indifferent.
===
the last form is called the *contrary*, or more emphatically the *opposite*, is usually indifferent.
>>>

<<<
But to apply the opposite to a mere contrary (c.g. to I did not hit him in relation to I hit him, which has no opposite), or to the
===
But to apply *the opposite* to a mere contrary (e.g. to *I did not hit him* in relation to *I hit him*, which has no opposite), or to the
>>>

<<<
converse (e.g. to He hit me in relation to I hit him, to which it is neither contrary nor opposite), is a looseness that may easily result in | misunderstanding;
===
converse (e.g. to *He hit me* in relation to *I hit him*, to which it is neither contrary nor opposite), is a looseness that may easily result in misunderstanding;
>>>

<<<
the temptation to go wrong is intelligible when it is remembered that with certain types of sentence (A exceeds B) the con- | verse & the opposite are identical (B exceeds A).
===
the temptation to go wrong is intelligible when it is remembered that with certain types of sentence (*A exceeds B*) the converse & the opposite are identical (*B exceeds A*).
>>>

<<<
contrast. 1. Pronounce the noun k6’ntrast, the verb kontri’st; see Noun & VERB ACCENT.
===
**contrast.** 1. Pronounce the noun kŏ'ntrăst, the verb kontră'st; see ^^NOUN^^ & ^^VERB ACCENT^^.
>>>

<<<
2. The transitive use of the verb with one | of the contrasted things as subject, in the sense be a c. to or set off by c., was formerly common, but in | modern writing is either an archaism ‘or a blunder; with should always be inserted.
===
2. The transitive use of the verb with one of the contrasted things as subject, in the sense *be a c. to* or *set off by c.*, was formerly common, but in modern writing is either an archaism or a blunder; *with* should always be inserted.
>>>

<<<
The use meant is seen in:—The sun-tinged hermit & the pale elder c. cach other./ Monks whose | dark garments contrasted the snow./ | The smooth
===
The use meant is seen in:—*The sun-tinged hermit & the pale elder c. each other.*/*Monks whose dark garments contrasted the snow.*/*The smooth
>>>

<<<
slopes are contrasted by the aspect of the country on the opposite bank. control makes -lled, -llable, &c.; f see -LL-, -L-.
===
slopes are contrasted by the aspect of the country on the opposite bank.*

**control** makes *-lled, -llable,* &c.; see ^^-LL-^^, ^^-L-^^.
>>>

